1. Take a 12-byte write IV and a 64-bit record sequence number.
2. Produce the 12-byte per-record nonce as one aggregate.
3. Produce the 16-byte pre-counter block J0 as one aggregate.
4. Take the byte length of the inner plaintext (content, type, padding).
5. Produce the 5-byte record header, which is the AEAD's additional data.
6. Take the byte lengths of the additional data and of the ciphertext.
7. Produce the 16-byte GHASH length block as one aggregate.
8. Take the GHASH output S and the encrypted pre-counter block E_K(J0).
9. Produce the 16-byte authentication tag as one aggregate.
10. Take two 16-byte tags: the one computed and the one received.
11. The nonce: XOR the IV with the sequence number written big-endian and
    right-aligned, so the first four IV bytes pass through unchanged.
12. J0: bind the nonce aggregate and append the four bytes 00 00 00 01.
13. The record header: type 23, version 3.3, then the inner length plus
    16 for the tag as a 16-bit big-endian number.
14. The length block: the AAD length and the ciphertext length, each
    times eight (bits), each as a 64-bit big-endian number.
15. The tag: XOR S with E_K(J0), byte by byte.
16. The compare: fold every byte's difference into one accumulator with
    OR and XOR only -- no early exit -- and answer 1 iff it is zero.
